From: Luma Developers
Subject: [PATCH] Keycap key path: no stall, commit at once, press-order input

The on-screen keyboard's key path rebuilt after an on-device investigation
(Pixel 9 Pro Fold, 360x808 at scale 3), where each press or release made
the next frame take about 270 ms:

- keyboardBox no longer tracks hover. A touch entered and left it on every
  key, and the :hover change restyled the whole keyboard, so St re-rendered
  the keyboard's CPU-blurred inset background (Keycap sheet) twice per tap.
  The open keyboard is never restyled or resized per key: _relayout() writes
  only what changed, open() on a visible keyboard does nothing, the key
  preview changes only its text, translation and opacity, the suggestion
  strip reuses its buttons, and the pressed-cap, preview and letter layers
  are drawn once, nearly transparent, after the keyboard opens.
- A key's whole cell is its target. One pointer tracker on the keyboard
  owns input (caps are drawn only): taps in gutters and margins go to the
  nearest key after a learned per-row touch correction, and near a letter
  boundary the word's prefix mass can move the boundary by at most a fifth
  of a key. A new press first finishes older held keys, so overlapping taps
  type in press order.
- A release commits at once at default priority, one commit per key event;
  a second key in the same main-loop turn follows Mutter's text-input done
  (CLUTTER_PRIORITY_EVENTS + 2), never at idle priority. Shell entries (the
  unlock password, search) take text synchronously. Typing therefore works
  while the frame clock keeps the main loop busy.
- Long press and delete repeat are decided at frame time from event
  timestamps, never by a GLib timer alone, and a repeat is bound to a live
  touch: release, cancel, a grab taking the touch (seen by an event filter),
  unmap, hide, focus loss and a layer change end it.
- Alternatives open on the held key with no stage grab; the release types
  the chosen one, and a menu opened from the strip passes on the press that
  closes it, so no popup swallows a tap.
- Sentence case is decided from the keyboard's own view of the text before
  the caret (application reports that echo its sends are ignored), and the
  layer never changes while a key is down. Shift's double tap and the
  double-space period use event times.
- In Mobile and Tablet the keyboard keeps its height clear through Mutter's
  input panel inset, so applications shrink above it (Android adjustResize)
  with the keyboard colour underneath while they resize; Desktop and the
  Shell's own entries keep the window slide.
- Haptics call feedbackd's TriggerFeedback with D-Bus activation and back
  off quietly when it is missing or fails.
- With a keyboard-layout (xkb) input source, IBus is not sent the caret
  rectangle or surrounding text on every keystroke, and typing-booster is
  no longer preloaded.

